---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

import PairView from "../components/PairView.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

const chapters = await getCollection("chapters");
console.log("Chapters:", chapters);
---

<SiteLayout>
    <div class="space-y-8">
        <h1>Python to Excel Knowledge Transfer</h1>

        <div class="space-y-4">
            <p>
                This site helps Python programmers learn Excel by showing
                side-by-side examples. It is organized into chapters consisting
                of lessons. Each lesson is a pair that covers the same topic in
                both Python and Excel.
            </p>

            <div class="space-y-2 p-4 border-l-4 border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50">
                <p>
                    For example, in in the first few lessions we'll cover common
                    functions, logical operations, and control flow. You might
                    see:
                </p>

                <PairView pair="concat-basics" left="python" />
            </div>
        </div>

        <section class="space-y-3">
            <h2>Conventions</h2>
            <p>
                In each Excel pair we present a small table to make formulas
                explicit. Read the columns as:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
                <li>
                    <strong>Cell</strong>: the address you should click or type
                    in (e.g., <code>A1</code>, <code>B2</code>).
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Value / Formula</strong>: literals appear as numbers
                    or text; formulas start with
                    <code>=</code>. When you see <code>&rarr;</code>, the value
                    to the right is the expected result after pressing <kbd
                        >Enter</kbd
                    >.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Logical results display as <code>TRUE</code> / <code
                        >FALSE</code
                    > to match Excel’s capitalization.
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The Python snippets run against simple variables so you can copy
                them into a REPL. For Excel, type the listed values into the
                matching cells and then enter the formula to see the same
                outcome.
            </p>
            <div class="rounded-md border border-zinc-200 p-3 bg-white">
                <h3 class="text-base font-semibold">Example</h3>
                <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-zinc-600">
                    A basic addition exercise would appear as:
                </p>
                <table class="mt-2 w-full text-left text-sm">
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th
                                class="border-b border-zinc-200 pb-1 pr-4 font-medium text-zinc-500"
                            >
                                Cell
                            </th>
                            <th
                                class="border-b border-zinc-200 pb-1 font-medium text-zinc-500"
                            >
                                Value / Formula
                            </th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td
                                class="py-1 pr-4 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-800"
                                >A1</td
                            >
                            <td class="py-1 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-800"
                                >12</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td
                                class="py-1 pr-4 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-800"
                                >B1</td
                            >
                            <td class="py-1 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-800"
                                >8</td
                            >
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td
                                class="py-1 pr-4 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-800"
                                >C1</td
                            >
                            <td class="py-1 font-mono text-sm text-zinc-800">
                                =A1 + B1 → <span class="font-semibold">20</span>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <div>
            <h2>Table of Contents</h2>
            <ol>
                {
                    chapters
                        .toSorted((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order)
                        .map((chapter) => (
                            <li>
                                <a
                                    href={`/learn-excel-via-python-knowledge/chapters/${chapter.id}/`}
                                    class="text-blue-600 hover:underline"
                                >
                                    {chapter.data.title}
                                </a>
                            </li>
                        ))
                }
            </ol>
        </div>
    </div>
</SiteLayout>
